# {{ title }}

YAMD has two ways to mark content as a flashcard. The extension collects them from every note and schedules reviews using SM-2 — the same algorithm behind Anki. The schedule is stored in `.yamd/srs.json` at the workspace root, so it travels with the notes when you share or commit the folder.

::toc{depth=2}

---

## Container card

A `:::card` block separates the question from the answer with a `---` line.
This is the richest format — you can put any YAMD content on either side.

:::card {#card:binary-search}
What is the time complexity of binary search, and why?

---

**$O(\log n)$**

Each comparison halves the search space, so after $k$ comparisons at most $n / 2^k$ elements remain. Solving $n / 2^k = 1$ gives $k = \log_2 n$.
:::

A card with no `---` separator between the question and the answer triggers a warning instead:

```yamd
:::card
A card with no --- separator produces a warning.
:::
```

The linter reports **Y605**, "This card has no answer," so you won't forget to add one.

---

## Short cards

A one-line Q&A, like vocabulary or a formula, is just a short container card —
there's no separate one-liner syntax, since a short card is just a short `:::card`.

:::card
What does "idempotent" mean?

---

Applying the operation more than once has the same effect as once.
:::

:::card
State the product rule.

---

$(fg)' = f'g + fg'$
:::

:::card
What is the capital of France?

---

Paris
:::

---

## Cloze deletion

Wrap the hidden word or phrase in `:cloze[…]`. Each `:cloze` in a paragraph becomes a separate card. When you review, one blank is shown at a time.

The :cloze[mitochondrion] is the :cloze[powerhouse] of the cell.

The derivative of :cloze[$\sin x$] is :cloze[$\cos x$].

**Named groups** let two clozes in the same paragraph belong together — useful when the blanks are related:

The :cloze[First World War]{n=1} ended in :cloze[1918]{n=1}.

---

## IDs and stability

Every card has a stable ID so the schedule survives edits elsewhere in the note:
- An explicit `{#id}` on a container card: `:::card {#card:my-id}`
- A hash of the question text for leaf cards and cloze paragraphs (auto-generated)

Changing the question text creates a new ID and a fresh scheduling entry. If you rename many cards at once, run **YAMD: Prune Orphaned Card Data** to remove stale entries from `srs.json`.

---

## Reviewing cards

Open the command palette and run **YAMD: Review Flashcards**.

- Due cards (oldest due date first) are shown, then up to `yamd.study.newCardsPerDay` new ones (default 20).
- Grade each card: **Again** (forgot), **Hard**, **Good**, **Easy**.
- The interval shown on each button is the SM-2 prediction for that grade.
- After grading, click **Open in editor** to jump to the source line.

Settings:

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
| ------- | ------- | ------- |
| `yamd.study.newCardsPerDay` | `20` | New cards shown per session |

---

## Quiz mode

Run **YAMD: Toggle Quiz Mode** while a preview is open. Cloze answers are hidden — the preview shows `[…]` — and clicking one (or pressing :kbd[Enter] / :kbd[Space] while it is focused) reveals the answer. This turns any note into a self-test without touching the source.

---

## Tips

- Put flashcards at the end of a lecture note under a **Review cards** heading.
- Use container cards for multi-line proofs or code snippets.
- Use leaf cards for vocabulary, definitions and short formulae.
- Use cloze on sentences you've already written — just wrap the key words.
- Keep the question and answer short enough to read in 10 seconds.
